All of the now-current icon set are based on a template of one of the first 
implementations as in the previous attempt.  Even a klutz like me can make 
variations that have some mathematical semblance to them using Junior Icon 
Editor, an acceptable freeware tool.  I rate it pretty low from the 
"artistic" sense but at least this way we have a clear "theme" to tie 
together function with position.

The main point here is this is still a SERVING SUGGESTION.  Place them 
wherever makes the most sense to you, or give up on medium icons altogether 
if that floats your goat.  I can only say that having repeatability with 
the added visual cue of color consistency helps to make the process less 
tedious so you can concentrate on using them without having to rederive 
them each time.  I have the advantage of experience with this sort of 
thing; it does help, but there is no accounting for taste, even mine!

In any case, the main points about Windows 10 apply:

1) You cannot turn off auto-sorting and snap to a grid in Windows 10 except 
on the desktop, but that means you have your machine sort-of dedicated to 
PEPS.  Windows ought to not require that, but Microsoft lost their way in 
Windows 10; this method DID work but many features were lost by crude 
brute-force updates, which seems to be the "new order" for Windows, which 
is frankly pathetic.

2) Once you can turn off auto-sorting, aligning to a grid is not half bad.  
The eyes seek some level of order;  Those even more "artistically inclined" 
can even defeat that (but not in Windows 10)

3) An ugly alternative "native" to Windows 10 can be kludged by using some 
other of the available icon options; I haven't worked on it yet, just in 
theory.  But the point is that if the caption label of an icon starts with 
a sequence number, this should be able to defeat the auto-sort by making it 
do what we want.  The catch is you have to then create dummy icons with the 
numbers of faked place-holders, but there are some tricks to work on to 
help (I have to research if transparency tricks apply, then you at least 
won't have to see them, but inevitably, the visible icons do need to have 
something that the auto-sorting will accept].

4) There are a few subordinate windows, primarily to avoid clutter on the 
main Command window.  The compromise introduced by Windows 10 may be 
passable merely because there generally aren't all that many icons in these 
subordinate windows.

I will spend some more time on this, but not that much.  Once this aspect 
of the package is at least sanely stable, then I can finally document all 
that is, and not an infinitely moving target. etc.

Since it is clear the desired sub-options do not work past Windows 8.1, I 
have to perfect another BATCH script that points out what can be done, and 
asks to perform that which can be done:

a) In Windows XP and Windows Vista, there are commands to just enable the 
align to grid and separately turn off auto-arrange.  On this point alone, 
these were the last rational releases.

b) Windows 7 and Windows 8/8.1 are released without this capability, but 
with a few registry tricks, they can be enabled just the same as in the 
earlier releases.  Thus, a more elaborate setup option can handle all of 
this properly.

c) Windows 10 is hopeless so the only thing that can be done is detecting 
the O/S is Win10, inform the user with some appropriate messages, but 
otherwise do nothing; of course recommend one of the emulators, preferably 
for Windows 7.  Install the package inside of the emulator and then b) or 
a) above will apply, etc.

This entire debacle has taken far too much time better spent on the more 
important aspects of the PEPS package, but with Microsoft ending support 
for Windows 7 shortly (well,there is always a way, but then you are going 
off into the Windows weeds and not staying on target for a good fully 
functional PDP-8 emulator and development package), this has become 
somewhat forced upon me.  Many are angry at Microsoft for all sorts of 
reasons, many beyond the scope of this project, but we are all in the same 
proverbial boat.

cjl


On Thursday, November 7, 2019 at 2:13:24 PM UTC-5, clasystems wrote:
>
> This is likely the template for a hopefully soon release.  At least all 
> the icons have a similar, albeit simplistic, theme for the icons.  Colors 
> are chosen to hopefully instill a sense of order and functionality.  Actual 
> color choice is somewhat arbitrary, but I will entertain any realistic 
> variations or color swaps.  I've tried to use colors in a way consistent 
> with ordinary real-world experience.
>
> All critiques of the new Command Window are welcome.
>
> More importantly, only a few items do not work as advertises. (None of 
> them are in the main command window and only affect users on certain 
> Windows releases.)
>
> It still appears that Windows 10 will be either a kludge unacceptable to 
> some, or use on of the available emulators, preferably of Windows 7.
>
> As an added bonus, there are linux versions of the Win7 emulator thus more 
> people should be able to use the PEPS package, etc.
>
> cjl
>

